WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.179
WT Stead, the Father of Investigative Journalism

00:00:01.179 --> 00:00:04.420
Victorian era Britain was a time of massive
change.

00:00:04.420 --> 00:00:09.460
From sweeping imperialist expansion abroad
to political and social reform at home, to

00:00:09.460 --> 00:00:15.900
the rise of mysticism, spiritualism, and romanticism,
the nation was growing and, in many ways,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:16.900
thriving.

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:22.410
For some, cities were becoming bustling, prosperous
hubs of industry, but for the less fortunate,

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:26.860
they also became cesspools of disease, darkness,
and misery.

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:31.680
It was an era of invention and advancement,
and Britain's citizens learned about it through

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:32.980
the newspaper.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.600
Before the modern era of journalism, there
was once a time when the newspaper was simply

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.090
for reporting the news.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:43.539
However, a young editor named WT Stead saw
the potential for it to be something more

00:00:43.539 --> 00:00:49.380
-- at one point, he described his vision to
a friend as "a glorious opportunity of attacking

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:50.380
the devil."

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:51.380
And he did.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:56.719
There were plenty of devils out there, rearing
their ugly heads during this time of all-encompassing

00:00:56.719 --> 00:00:57.719
change.

00:00:57.719 --> 00:01:02.289
Not only did he attack them, but people listened,
and he proved that one person really can be

00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:06.250
the voice that changes history.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:08.510
This is Stead’s story.

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:20.310
TITLE: A world shaped by the newspaper

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:25.510
Today's world is connected in ways the average
Victorian-era citizen could never have believed.

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.140
News travels fast, they say, and today, that's
certainly true.

00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:34.710
It was quite a bit less true in the 19th century,
so when newspapers became widely accessible

00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:39.970
in the mid-1800s, it quickly shaped the daily
operation of entire cities.

00:01:39.970 --> 00:01:44.380
Typically, newspapers were the only way people
would hear about wars in far-away lands or

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:47.110
the goings-on of their own governments.

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:51.980
That made them powerful, and many built massive
and ornate buildings that towered over the

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.850
worlds they reported on.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:57.710
Since not everyone could afford to buy their
own paper, public social clubs were formed

00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:02.800
and reading rooms were built, where hundreds
of members could get together to share papers,

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.740
read the news, and discuss.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.200
Others headed to the pubs, where landlords
kept papers on hand and sometimes paid speakers

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.739
to perform the news to raucous applause.

00:02:11.739 --> 00:02:15.750
There were more newspapers in public libraries
than there were books, newsagents popped up

00:02:15.750 --> 00:02:20.430
everywhere, and newsboys hawked the latest
edition with the help of signs proclaiming

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.720
the day's headlines.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:26.930
Suddenly, everyone was learning about the
world, and they only wanted more.

00:02:26.930 --> 00:02:31.900
William Thomas Stead was brought into that
rapidly changing world on July 5, 1849.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:35.550
Born a minister's son, he could read both
Latin and English by the time he was five

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:40.219
years old, and it would be his words that
would not only shape his own life, but go

00:02:40.219 --> 00:02:42.319
on to change the world he lived in.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.360
Even though it had originally been assumed
that he would follow in his father's footsteps

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:49.419
and become a minister, that's not what happened
at all.

00:02:49.419 --> 00:02:53.389
After finishing his schooling, he ended up
working as a clerk in a merchant's office

00:02:53.389 --> 00:02:54.530
in Newcastle.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:58.489
While he was there, he was a regular contributor
to a newspaper called the Northern Echo.

00:02:58.489 --> 00:03:03.559
The editor, John Copleston, saw something
valuable in him and mentored him for a surprisingly

00:03:03.559 --> 00:03:04.659
short time.

00:03:04.659 --> 00:03:09.769
The paper opened in 1870, Stead got his foot
in the door, and in 1871, the owner of the

00:03:09.769 --> 00:03:11.879
paper offered him his mentor's job.

00:03:11.879 --> 00:03:15.340
At the time, Stead had never even stepped
into the paper's offices.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:19.269
Stead hesitated, as he was loathe to take
the job of a man who had been such an inspirational

00:03:19.269 --> 00:03:20.760
teacher and mentor to him.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:25.040
But when Copleston left voluntarily, he rose
to the challenge, becoming the country's youngest

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.040
newspaper editor.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:31.079
It was a huge task, and to Stead, it was a
chance to make a monumental contribution to

00:03:31.079 --> 00:03:32.079
society.

00:03:32.079 --> 00:03:37.719
He wrote, "I felt the sacredness of the power
placed in my hands, to be used on behalf of

00:03:37.719 --> 00:03:40.949
the poor, the outcast, and the oppressed."

00:03:40.949 --> 00:03:42.549
And that's exactly what he did.

00:03:42.549 --> 00:03:46.870
His goal was to create a newspaper that was
just as entertaining as it was informative,

00:03:46.870 --> 00:03:49.749
one that would reach the masses and spur them
to action.

00:03:49.749 --> 00:03:54.870
He started out in the most shocking way possible,
with a topic he'd revisit later: prostitution.

00:03:54.870 --> 00:04:00.150
He called it "the ghastliest curse which haunts
civilised society," and at the time he was

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:04.609
splashing it across the front page, it was
such a taboo subject that talking about it

00:04:04.609 --> 00:04:07.799
was just as illicit as partaking in it.

00:04:07.799 --> 00:04:12.200
Stead's condemnation of prostitution wasn't
quite universal, though -- at the same time

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:17.019
he condemned the wealthy echelons of society
for funding brothels, he acknowledged that,

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:20.489
for many, it was the only way they could put
food on the table.

00:04:20.489 --> 00:04:24.389
Stead's preaching from the pulpit and his
almost sermon-like articles set the tone for

00:04:24.389 --> 00:04:26.900
the Northern Echo and for the rest of his
career.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.610
He condemned entire nations for their politics
and their human rights abuses, and he waxed

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:35.060
philosophical on why the death penalty was
a necessary evil.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:39.659
That was a viewpoint that was a bit problematic,
considering the paper was funded by Quakers,

00:04:39.659 --> 00:04:43.990
a religious group who believed there is a
bit of God in everyone, and that all life

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:44.990
is valuable.

00:04:44.990 --> 00:04:49.189
It wasn't long before Stead grew too big for
the Northern Echo, and headed to London to

00:04:49.189 --> 00:04:52.340
take up with The Pall Mall Gazette.

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:57.770
TITLE: New Journalism

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:01.860
Stead continued his fire-and-brimstone flavor
of journalism when he got to London, but it

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:03.300
wasn't particularly popular.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:09.360
Some of his contemporaries hated him as much
as he hated London, and no one made a secret

00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:10.360
of it.

00:05:10.360 --> 00:05:13.280
At the same time Stead called the bustling
metropolis "the grave of all earnestness",

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:18.240
his paper was nicknamed the "Dunghill Gazette"
by poet Algernon Swinburne.

00:05:18.240 --> 00:05:23.159
Novelist Mathew Arnold was more direct, calling
Stead's sensationalist style "feather-brained".

00:05:23.159 --> 00:05:27.790
But as much hate as he got, he also got results.

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:33.770
In 1883, the clergyman Andrew Mearns wrote
a pamphlet called "The Bitter Cry of Outcast

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:34.770
London".

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:38.500
It detailed the plight of the countless souls
living in the slums of the city, and it was

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.610
dire stuff.

00:05:39.610 --> 00:05:40.610
He wrote:

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:45.039
"Few who will read these pages will have any
conception of what these pestilential human

00:05:45.039 --> 00:05:50.010
rookeries are, where tens of thousands are
crowded together amidst horrors which call

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:53.849
to mind what we have heard of the middle passage
of the slave ship.

00:05:53.849 --> 00:05:59.159
To get to them you have to penetrate courts
reeking with poisonous and malodorous gases

00:05:59.159 --> 00:06:05.479
arising from accumulations of sewage and refuse
scattered in all directions and often flowing

00:06:05.479 --> 00:06:09.999
beneath your feet; courts, many of them which
the sun never penetrates, which are never

00:06:09.999 --> 00:06:15.319
visited by a breath of fresh air, and which
rarely know the virtues of a drop of cleansing

00:06:15.319 --> 00:06:16.319
water."

00:06:16.319 --> 00:06:19.419
If it wasn't for Stead, the pamphlet might
have gone unnoticed.

00:06:19.419 --> 00:06:24.189
But the journalist picked it up and ran with
it, using The Pall Mall Gazette as a megaphone

00:06:24.189 --> 00:06:25.919
for Mearns’ monologue.

00:06:25.919 --> 00:06:31.139
Consequently, the paper increased its reputation
for sensationalism and scandalous stories,

00:06:31.139 --> 00:06:36.320
as its readers were outraged at the lurid
tales about what missionaries would find in

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.409
the slums of London.

00:06:37.409 --> 00:06:42.650
The paper spoke of dozens of people, huddled
together in the dirt and squalor, of children

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:49.069
poisoned by the foul stench, of child corpses
and vermin abandoned to rot the darkness.

00:06:49.069 --> 00:06:53.220
The outrage of these freshsly realized details
didn't just disappear.

00:06:53.220 --> 00:06:58.310
Mearns' words and Stead's relentless promotion
of the pamphlet resulted in new housing legislation

00:06:58.310 --> 00:07:03.020
and the organization of missions throughout
London, whose only goal was to alleviate some

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:06.919
of the suffering and misery described in the
macabre tales.

00:07:06.919 --> 00:07:10.280
The next year, Stead picked up another cause:
the Navy.

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:14.530
Calling for an end to party squabbles in the
interest of national security, he appealed

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:19.300
to the government to make sure its fighting
fleet was, indeed, capable of fighting.

00:07:19.300 --> 00:07:24.250
The result was the government making a £3.5
million investment into upgrading its fleet,

00:07:24.250 --> 00:07:26.110
a staggering amount for the time.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:27.110
Clearly, readers were listening.

00:07:27.110 --> 00:07:36.090
TITLE: The Maiden Tribute of Modern Babylon

00:07:36.090 --> 00:07:41.009
Stead's tour de force came in 1885, published
under the sensationalized headline "The Maiden

00:07:41.009 --> 00:07:42.349
Tribute of Babylon."

00:07:42.349 --> 00:07:47.389
This is when he returned to a subject that
he had condemned earlier — prostitution.

00:07:47.389 --> 00:07:51.940
This time, he was kicking the door wide open
on one of the darkest areas of London's seedy

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:56.900
underbelly, and that was the selling of virgin
children to men who were wealthy &amp; debased

00:07:56.900 --> 00:08:02.080
enough that they found no greater pleasure
in life than [quote] "the exclusive luxury

00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:06.070
of revelling in the cries of an immature child."

00:08:06.070 --> 00:08:10.400
Stead didn't just write about it second-hand
-- he spent four weeks in the streets, brothels,

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:16.990
and hospitals of London, meeting with everyone
from doctors and bishops to those deemed "procuresses."

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:21.790
These were people willing to find and broker
deals for human flesh and services.

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:26.110
He thoroughly documented all that he found,
describing the London underworld as a place

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:31.330
"with all the vices of Gomorrah, daring the
vengeance of long-suffering Heaven."

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:35.469
The story that unfolded on the pages of The
Pall Mall Gazette was horrifying.

00:08:35.469 --> 00:08:39.000
Stead secured a meeting with a contact who
was connected to all sorts of crime in the

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.000
city's underbelly.

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:44.060
When he inquired about purchasing a virgin
girl for his pleasure, he was told that wouldn't

00:08:44.060 --> 00:08:45.060
be a problem.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:49.380
When Stead began to ask more questions about
any potential consequences attached to his

00:08:49.380 --> 00:08:54.240
actions, he was further assured that screams
would not be investigated and legal action

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:55.800
would not be pursued.

00:08:55.800 --> 00:09:00.339
He quoted his source as saying, "Whom is she
to prosecute? … who would believe her?"

00:09:00.339 --> 00:09:05.450
Stead, incredulous, asked for confirmation
that these unspeakable procurements were common,

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:10.370
and was not only assured that they were, but
was told, "And you cannot help it, as long

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:15.350
as men have money, procuresses are skillful,
and women are weak and inexperienced."

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:19.870
It was something the stalwart journalist could
not walk away from, and could not let continue

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:22.910
to happen while he stood idly by.

00:09:22.910 --> 00:09:27.120
But just the word of a seedy soul prowling
the shadows of London wasn't going to be enough

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:30.100
to instigate the change Stead wanted to see.

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:35.540
He knew he needed proof, and started by finding
and interviewing former brothel owners and

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:37.570
those whose innocence had been sold.

00:09:37.570 --> 00:09:41.910
It wasn't long before he met a working brothel-keeper
who promised it would only take him only a

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:47.779
few days to find several virgin girls — whose
untouched condition was verified by a doctor.

00:09:47.779 --> 00:09:51.550
Stead backed away from communication with
this man, but it wasn't long before he found

00:09:51.550 --> 00:09:57.250
one of these virgin girls, grown and turned
procuress, who was willing to reach out to

00:09:57.250 --> 00:10:00.620
old contacts and find him the sort of girl
he was asking for.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:01.620
The price?

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:02.620
£3 to £5.

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:07.410
As Stead waded deeper, he found that crude
medical procedures and violent assaults didn't

00:10:07.410 --> 00:10:10.709
bring an end to the nightmare the children
were sold into.

00:10:10.709 --> 00:10:15.170
Many children, most of whom were orphans or
had no family to speak of, were generally

00:10:15.170 --> 00:10:20.120
passed along to brothel-owners to fill their
ranks, or ended up on the streets making a

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:21.529
living any way they could.

00:10:21.529 --> 00:10:25.720
And there were plenty of options, as Stead
would write: "Anything can be done for money,

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.759
if you only know where to take it."

00:10:27.759 --> 00:10:32.460
Stead was careful to say that he knew everything
he said was true, that it wasn't just sensationalist

00:10:32.460 --> 00:10:38.340
nonsense… because he oversaw the negotiation
and purchase of a 13-year-old girl.

00:10:38.340 --> 00:10:43.010
Her name was Lily, and she had a family who
agreed to sell her as a household servant.

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:46.680
A procuress paid her parents £5, and she
bid them goodbye.

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:51.950
She was washed and dressed, and Stead's description
of her makes it somehow even more heartbreaking.

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:56.570
She could read and write, he wrote of her
as "a loving, affectionate child, whose kindly

00:10:56.570 --> 00:11:01.610
feeling for the drunken mother who had sold
her into nameless infamy was very touching

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:02.610
to behold."

00:11:02.610 --> 00:11:07.130
She was "full of delight at going to her new
situation," he wrote, stressing that even

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:11.509
though her parents knew what was going to
happen to her, she hadn't a clue.

00:11:11.509 --> 00:11:16.750
Her status as a virgin was verified by a midwife,
the purchaser was supplied with some chloroform,

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:20.579
and Stead recorded everything — including
the names of the people involved.

00:11:20.579 --> 00:11:22.601
He immediately published the story.

00:11:22.601 --> 00:11:27.000
That public was outraged, and Stead got all
the reaction he could have hoped for… and

00:11:27.000 --> 00:11:28.000
then, a little bit more.

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:32.290
On a national level, the expose sparked the
passing of the Criminal Law Amendment Act

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:36.670
of 1885, which laid the groundwork for other
legislature designed to protect women and

00:11:36.670 --> 00:11:41.649
girls who were forced into prostitution, and
it also raised the age of sexual consent from

00:11:41.649 --> 00:11:43.970
13 to 16 years old.

00:11:43.970 --> 00:11:47.389
For the part he played, Stead found himself
in front of a judge, brought up on charges

00:11:47.389 --> 00:11:49.550
of abduction and indecent assault.

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:53.830
When Justice Henry Charles Lopes handed out
the sentence, he explained that Stead's experiment

00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:58.759
had failed, because he hadn't bought a child
for exploitation at all… he had rescued

00:11:58.759 --> 00:12:01.589
her before anything bad had actually happened
to her.

00:12:01.589 --> 00:12:06.630
And that meant he had published what the judge
ruled "a distorted account of the case", and

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:08.519
called it "a disgrace to journalism."

00:12:08.519 --> 00:12:13.529
Stead was sentenced to six months' jail and
hard labor, a sentence backed up by the fact

00:12:13.529 --> 00:12:18.140
he had technically broken the law by getting
only her mother's permission to buy her, not

00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:19.339
her father's as well.

00:12:19.339 --> 00:12:23.670
The sentence was considered less about the
law and more about what Stead had done.

00:12:23.670 --> 00:12:28.269
Mostly, his jailtime was thanks to the people
who truly hated him — the wealthy men in

00:12:28.269 --> 00:12:34.920
power, the ones whose actions he had a tendency
to condemn in a humiliatingly public way.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:40.410
TITLE: The eccentric nature of brilliance

00:12:40.410 --> 00:12:44.001
After Stead's stint in jail, he was never
quite the powerhouse of journalism he had

00:12:44.001 --> 00:12:45.001
been.

00:12:45.001 --> 00:12:49.029
That’s not entirely because of how his expose
on child prostitution ended, though; in the

00:12:49.029 --> 00:12:51.220
grand scheme of things, it was a huge success.

00:12:51.220 --> 00:12:55.769
But for years, he had preached some unpopular
opinions, including advocating for Ireland’s

00:12:55.769 --> 00:13:00.560
self-governance, as well as forming closer
ties with both the U.S. and Tsarist Russia.

00:13:00.560 --> 00:13:05.100
When he took over at The Pall Mall Gazette,
he had decreased his popularity by doing something

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:10.440
revolutionary: introducing decidedly American
features into British papers, like large-print

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:14.020
headlines, bylines for his reporters, and
celebrity interviews.

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:17.980
There were a few other things Stead did that
raised some eyebrows, including offering his

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:23.240
unwavering support to the establishment of
Esperanto as a global language, vocalizing

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:28.170
his opposition to British military endeavors
like the Second Boer War, and suggesting that

00:13:28.170 --> 00:13:32.970
the nations of the world needed to form something
he dubbed Union International, which would

00:13:32.970 --> 00:13:37.759
have been a version of the United Nations
dedicated strictly to global peacekeeping

00:13:37.759 --> 00:13:39.379
and anti-militarism.

00:13:39.379 --> 00:13:44.750
But it was actually an element of his personal
life that eventually gave 19th century Britian

00:13:44.750 --> 00:13:50.149
its biggest pause, as Stead was also a devoted
spiritualist who may have been a little too

00:13:50.149 --> 00:13:52.100
radical, even for Victorian Britain.

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:56.079
And that's saying something -- the spiritualism
movement had been wildly popular since the

00:13:56.079 --> 00:14:01.350
Fox sisters claimed to have channeled the
spirit of a murdered man in 1848 and established

00:14:01.350 --> 00:14:02.459
contact with the other side.

00:14:02.459 --> 00:14:07.129
The idea that it was possible to contact loved
ones who had passed on was obviously enticing,

00:14:07.129 --> 00:14:11.899
and it gained some serious traction within
a select sliver of the population, including

00:14:11.899 --> 00:14:12.899
Stead.

00:14:12.899 --> 00:14:17.440
Stead left The Pall Mall Gazette in 1890,
and founded not only The Review of Reviews,

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:20.209
but a spiritualist quarterly called Borderland.

00:14:20.209 --> 00:14:23.930
The publication was a sort of news outlet
for the supernatural, and included everything

00:14:23.930 --> 00:14:29.779
from astrological predictions and news stories
about ghosts and seances to reports on research

00:14:29.779 --> 00:14:31.149
conducted on the occult.

00:14:31.149 --> 00:14:35.329
For Stead, this wasn't a new fascination,
either, as he reportedly experienced some

00:14:35.329 --> 00:14:39.600
otherworldly phenomenon as a young man visiting
Hermitage Castle in Scotland.

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:44.889
It wasn't until 1892 that he saw someone practicing
automatic writing -- the idea that some men

00:14:44.889 --> 00:14:47.230
and women could channel spirits to write through
them.

00:14:47.230 --> 00:14:50.959
At first, he didn't subscribe to the idea,
but after giving it a try, he claimed to have

00:14:50.959 --> 00:14:53.940
made contact with an American journalist named
Julia Ames.

00:14:53.940 --> 00:14:58.149
Ames had died in 1891, not long after she
interviewed Stead.

00:14:58.149 --> 00:15:00.139
It didn't end with just making contact.

00:15:00.139 --> 00:15:04.089
Stead went on to write an entire book called
Letters From Julia, where he both received

00:15:04.089 --> 00:15:07.790
messages for a friend of the late journalist
and talked to her himself.

00:15:07.790 --> 00:15:12.329
They talked about grief, about the discovery
of the afterlife, of spiritual growth, of

00:15:12.329 --> 00:15:13.520
loss and of hope.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:17.779
Regardless of how inspirational the letters
were, Stead's obsession with spiritualism

00:15:17.779 --> 00:15:21.360
serious eroded his credibility in the journalistic
world.

00:15:21.360 --> 00:15:25.399
But if there's anything we know about Stead,
it's that he wasn't easily swayed by an unfavorable

00:15:25.399 --> 00:15:26.399
public opinion.

00:15:26.399 --> 00:15:30.410
He went on to found an organization called
Julia's Bureau, which was a group founded

00:15:30.410 --> 00:15:35.019
with the goal of, according to him, "attempting
to bridge the abyss between the Two Worlds."

00:15:35.019 --> 00:15:40.100
The idea, he said, was that when loved ones
passed on, they weren't just floating aimlessly

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.379
or off to sit on some clouds and play some
harps.

00:15:43.379 --> 00:15:47.079
They were more alive than they had ever been
before, and in desperate need of comforting

00:15:47.079 --> 00:15:50.100
the grieving souls who had been left behind.

00:15:50.100 --> 00:15:54.899
He appointed what he called "competent sensitives"
to the bureau, and their goal was a specific

00:15:54.899 --> 00:15:55.899
one.

00:15:55.899 --> 00:15:59.779
They were only to be used by living applicants
who were looking to reach out to those they

00:15:59.779 --> 00:16:01.700
had loved and lost.

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:06.260
Stead likened the bureau mediums to policemen
who reunited lost and frightened children

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:08.480
with their frantically searching mothers.

00:16:08.480 --> 00:16:13.079
And he wasn't doing it alone — Julia was
on the other side, he said, guiding his efforts.

00:16:13.079 --> 00:16:16.790
Julia's Bureau was only active for around
three years, and in that time, they worked

00:16:16.790 --> 00:16:20.100
with hundreds of grieving people and passed
on countless messages.

00:16:20.100 --> 00:16:24.839
It cost Stead around £1,500 pounds a year
to run, and it ended with his death.

00:16:24.839 --> 00:16:27.730
But that's not the end of his story.

00:16:27.730 --> 00:16:32.839
TITLE: Eerie prophecies and a sinking ship

00:16:32.839 --> 00:16:38.029
Toward the end of his life, Stead was derided
and ridiculed for his unwavering belief in

00:16:38.029 --> 00:16:39.029
spiritualism.

00:16:39.029 --> 00:16:42.920
And in all fairness, he certainly wasn't the
only believer — Sir Arthur Conan Doyle famously

00:16:42.920 --> 00:16:47.149
became a spiritualist, his beliefs cemented
in the face of the senseless loss of life

00:16:47.149 --> 00:16:48.740
that happened during the First World War.

00:16:48.740 --> 00:16:53.079
But sometimes it's only in hindsight that
certain things become clear, and in Stead's

00:16:53.079 --> 00:16:58.420
case, he penned some eerie tales that will
make even the staunchest skeptic think twice.

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:02.720
In 1886, Stead published a bit of cautionary
fiction in The Pall Mall Gazette.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:06.940
It was called "How the Mail Steamer went down
in Mid Atlantic, by a Survivor," and it was

00:17:06.940 --> 00:17:12.840
the story of a steamer ship carrying 916 passengers
when it collided with another vessel and sank.

00:17:12.840 --> 00:17:16.890
The story was in response to legislation that
dictated that the number of lifeboats a ship

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:21.770
needed to carry was based on the size of the
vessel, not on the number of passengers.

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:25.600
Stead described the sinking of the ship in
agonizing detail, narrating the hundreds of

00:17:25.600 --> 00:17:30.360
people who had been dumped into the water,
the narrator finding himself "amid a blackened,

00:17:30.360 --> 00:17:32.420
wriggling sheet of drowning creatures."

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:37.090
The tale ended with the footnote: "This is
exactly what might take place and what will

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:41.210
take place if the liners are sent to sea short
of boats."

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:45.620
Stead seemed almost obsessed with the idea,
and it wasn't the only time he wrote about

00:17:45.620 --> 00:17:46.720
tragedy at sea.

00:17:46.720 --> 00:17:51.190
In the 1892 Christmas edition of The Review
of Reviews, he wrote a tale called "From the

00:17:51.190 --> 00:17:56.600
Old World to the New," and told the story
of the White Star Liner Majestic, whose passengers

00:17:56.600 --> 00:18:02.130
witness a ship colliding with icebergs, sinking,
and ultimately returning to rescue the survivors.

00:18:02.130 --> 00:18:06.380
He described the sinking in vivid detail,
witnessed by one of the passengers on the

00:18:06.380 --> 00:18:08.890
nearby liner: "The waters bubbled and foamed.

00:18:08.890 --> 00:18:11.840
I could see the heads of a few swimmers in
the eddy.

00:18:11.840 --> 00:18:15.200
One after another they sank, and I saw them
no more."

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:19.200
Stead often predicted his own death would
come at the end of a hangman's noose, or by

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:20.200
drowning.

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:23.880
And not only was he right, but in hindsight,
that bit of fiction written from the point

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:28.060
of view of a man drowning amongst hundreds,
and his story about the White Star Liner Majestic

00:18:28.060 --> 00:18:32.400
and a daring rescue at sea after a collision
with an iceberg -- that was more prophetic

00:18:32.400 --> 00:18:34.420
than he possibly could have known.

00:18:34.420 --> 00:18:38.600
It was President William Howard Taft who requested
his presence at a Carnegie Hall peace congress

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:42.480
in 1912, and to get there, he booked passage
on the Titanic.

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:48.120
His first class ticket cost £26 11s; he charmed
other passengers with his stories before he

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:51.000
was ultimately among the lives claimed by
the disaster.

00:18:51.000 --> 00:18:55.190
It's unclear how he spent his last hours;
there are various witness testimonies that

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:58.710
claim to have seen him in the hours after
the Titanic's fateful collision.

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:02.470
Some claim to have seen him reading on the
stern of the ship as it rose into the night

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:07.120
sky; others say he spent his last hours sitting
in the First Class Smoking Room, and still

00:19:07.120 --> 00:19:11.250
others say he was spotted leading women and
children through the chaos and seeing them

00:19:11.250 --> 00:19:12.740
to the lifeboats.

00:19:12.740 --> 00:19:16.501
According to at least one testimony, taken
from a survivor named Phillip Mock, Stead

00:19:16.501 --> 00:19:20.580
actually had made it to a raft alongside Colonel
John Jacob Astor.

00:19:20.580 --> 00:19:24.100
They had been in the water, clinging to the
side, and Mock related, "Their feet became

00:19:24.100 --> 00:19:27.140
frozen, and they were compelled to release
their hold.

00:19:27.140 --> 00:19:28.140
Both were drowned."

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:32.380
Regardless of how he spent his last moments,
his Spiritualist associates would later claim

00:19:32.380 --> 00:19:37.110
he had reached out from the Great Beyond to
tell them of the tragedy, and that was how

00:19:37.110 --> 00:19:38.700
they first heard the news.

00:19:38.700 --> 00:19:41.070
His body was never recovered, or at least,
was never identified.

00:19:41.070 --> 00:19:45.481
If his earlier works had been prophetic, he
seemed not to have expected his end to come

00:19:45.481 --> 00:19:49.170
in the way he'd written -- at one point, he
had also written that he expected to be back

00:19:49.170 --> 00:20:09.910
home in London by May of 1912.

